<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-divergence-and-upper-lower-limits" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Divergence and upper/lower limits
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this problem, you will complete the proof of <xref ref="theorem-convergence-and-upper-and-lower-limits"/>. Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R </m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> diverges to <m> \infty </m> if and only if
        
        <md>
          \liminf_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = \infty
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We note that for a natural number <m> m \in \N </m> and a real number <m> B \in \R </m>, <m> x_{ n } \geq B </m> for all indices <m> n \geq m </m> if and only if
        
        <md>
          \inf_{ n \geq m }{ x_{ n } } \geq B
        </md>.
        
        First suppose that the sequence <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> diverges to <m> \infty </m>. Then for all real numbers <m> B \in \R </m>, there is a natural number <m> m_{ B } \in \N </m> so that <m> x_{ n } \gt B + 1 </m> for all indices <m> n \geq m_{ B } </m>. We observe that
        
        <md>
          \inf_{ n \geq m }{ x_{ n } } \geq \inf_{ n \geq m_{ B } }{ x_{ n } } \geq B + 1 \gt B
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> m \geq m_{ B } </m>, and so the sequence <m> \of{ \inf_{ n \geq m }{ x_{ n } } }_{ m = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> diverges to <m> \infty </m>; that is,
        
        <md>
          \liminf_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = \lim_{ m \to \infty }{ \inf_{ n \geq m }{ x_{ n } } } = \infty
        </md>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Conversely, now suppose that <m> \liminf_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = \infty </m>, and let <m> B \in \R </m> be a real number. Then there is a natural number <m> n_{ B } \in \N </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \inf_{ n \geq m }{ x_{ n } } \gt B
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> m \geq n_{ B } </m>. In particular,
        
        <md>
          x_{ n } \geq \inf_{ k \geq n_{ B } }{ x_{ k } } \gt B
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ B } </m>. Thus the sequence <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> diverges to <m> \infty </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> diverges to <m> -\infty </m> if and only if
        
        <md>
          \limsup_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = -\infty
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We note that for a natural number <m> m \in \N </m> and a real number <m> B \in \R </m>, <m> x_{ n } \leq B </m> for all indices <m> n \geq m </m> if and only if
        
        <md>
          \sup_{ n \geq m }{ x_{ n } } \leq B
        </md>.
        
        First suppose that the sequence <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> diverges to <m> -\infty </m>. Then for all real numbers <m> B \in \R </m>, there is a natural number <m> m_{ B } \in \N </m> so that <m> x_{ n } \lt B - 1 </m> for all indices <m> n \geq m_{ B } </m>. We observe that
        
        <md>
          \sup_{ n \geq m }{ x_{ n } } \leq \sup_{ n \geq m_{ B } }{ x_{ n } } \leq B - 1 \lt B
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> m \geq m_{ B } </m>, and so the sequence <m> \of{ \sup_{ n \geq m }{ x_{ n } } }_{ m = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> diverges to <m> -\infty </m>; that is,
        
        <md>
          \limsup_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = \lim_{ m \to \infty }{ \sup_{ n \geq m }{ x_{ n } } } = -\infty
        </md>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Conversely, now suppose that <m> \limsup_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = -\infty </m>, and let <m> B \in \R </m> be a real number. Then there is a natural number <m> n_{ B } \in \N </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \sup_{ n \geq m }{ x_{ n } } \lt B
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> m \geq n_{ B } </m>. In particular,
        
        <md>
          x_{ n } \leq \sup_{ k \geq n_{ B } }{ x_{ k } } \lt B
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ B } </m>. Thus the sequence <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> diverges to <m> -\infty </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>
